  "title": {
    "en": "Geese and swans"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "White-fronted, both Bean Geese, Pink-footed, Lesser White-fronted, Red-breasted, Whooper and Bewick's"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Winter in north-west Europe is goose time. From October to March, flocks from Siberia, Greenland, Iceland and Svalbard feed on grassland and fields. The grey geese (white-fronted, bean, pink-footed) look alike at a distance, and they differ in the bill, the legs, and the voice. A flock of thousands of geese is a good place to look for the rarer species.\n\nLearn the bill: the black-and-orange bill of the Bean Goose, the white blaze of the White-fronted, the small pink-banded bill of the Pink-footed. Then the legs: orange (White-fronted, Bean) or pink (Pink-footed). Finally the shape: Taiga Bean is long-necked and long-billed, Tundra Bean is more compact.\n\nThe two white swans are a classic pair: the Whooper Swan, with its big, yellow, wedge-shaped bill patch, and the Bewick's, smaller, with a rounder yellow patch. Both come from the north in late autumn. In winter listen: swans call across the fields at dusk."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "bill band"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A coloured band across the bill. The Pink-footed Goose has a pink one; Bean Geese have an orange one."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "blaze"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The white patch at the base of the bill, as on the White-fronted Goose."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "belly bars"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The black bars on the belly of an adult White-fronted Goose. No two birds are the same."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "wing tips"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The ends of the folded wings. In a Lesser White-fronted Goose they project beyond the tail."
      }
    }
  ],
